   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public officers law, in relation to the residence of peace officers employed as associate urban park rangers by a city with a population over one million",
    "basePrintNo": "S4315",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S4315-2011",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §3-b, Pub Off L",
    "lawSection": "Public Officers Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S4315\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\nAn act\nto amend the public officers law, in relation to the residence of peace\nofficers employed as associate urban park rangers by a city with a\npopulation over one million\n\nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\nThis bill would amend the public\nofficers Law to allow the hiring and continued employment associate\nurban park rangers by a city with a population over one million so\nlong as the employee has two years of service with the city and\nresides in a New York county contiguous with the employing\nmunicipality.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\nThe provisions allowing residence in a contiguous\ncounty are modeled after existing provisions of the public Officers\nLaw allowing members of municipal sanitation, fire, and corrections\ndepartments, and departments of health in any city over one million,\nto reside in a contiguous county. The same standards should apply to\nassociate urban park rangers. In 2009, the City of New York amended\nits residency requirement in Local Law 10 of 2009 to allow similarly\nrelaxed residency requirements i.e., two years of service with the\ncity of New York and residency in the counties of New York, Kings,\nQueens, Bronx, Richmond, Nassau, Westchester, Suffolk, Orange,\nRockland or Putnam - to dozens of titles represented by the same\ncollective bargaining agent that represents these titles. Because\nthese titles also hold peace officer status, however, Local Law 10\ndid not grant them equal rights because of 3-b of the Public\nOfficers Law, which requires peace officers to be residents of the\nsame county as the mayor, sheriff or other appointing official. This\nbill would simply give these titles the same opportunities as other\nemployees of the city of New York as well as members of municipal\nsanitation, fire, and corrections departments,\nand health departments in any city over one million.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\nNone.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\nNone.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately.\n\n",
